1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,115
Setting up new software is where good intentions go to die.

2
00:00:04,115 --> 00:00:10,811
You sign up, you get a blank screen, and somewhere there's a spreadsheet you're scared to touch.

3
00:00:10,811 --> 00:00:12,206
So let's just do it.

4
00:00:12,206 --> 00:00:18,344
In the next few minutes you'll set up your nonprofit and get your donors into GrantPipe.

5
00:00:18,344 --> 00:00:19,948
Three fields, one file.

6
00:00:19,948 --> 00:00:25,249
And before anything saves, you'll see exactly what GrantPipe is about to do.

7
00:00:25,249 --> 00:00:26,156
I built this.

8
00:00:26,156 --> 00:00:27,760
Let me show you around.

9
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,514
When you first sign in, GrantPipe asks you three things.

10
00:00:31,514 --> 00:00:32,586
Not a long form.

11
00:00:32,586 --> 00:00:32,989
Three.

12
00:00:32,989 --> 00:00:34,463
Your nonprofit's name.

13
00:00:34,463 --> 00:00:41,100
This is what shows up on your reports and exports, so spell it the way you want a funder to see it.

14
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:43,379
The month your fiscal year starts.

15
00:00:43,379 --> 00:00:46,529
Most nonprofits don't run on a January calendar

16
00:00:46,529 --> 00:00:50,753
and that's why year-end numbers can feel impossible to line up.

17
00:00:50,753 --> 00:00:54,775
Set it once here, and your dashboards and reports follow it.

18
00:00:54,775 --> 00:00:55,981
And your timezone.

19
00:00:55,981 --> 00:00:58,126
This one's quiet, but it counts.

20
00:00:58,126 --> 00:01:01,813
It's how GrantPipe knows when a deadline is really due.

21
00:01:01,813 --> 00:01:07,645
So a report doesn't get marked late just because some server runs on a different clock.

22
00:01:07,645 --> 00:01:09,187
That's the whole setup.

23
00:01:09,187 --> 00:01:11,600
Save it, and you've got a workspace.

24
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:12,540
Now your data.

25
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:17,709
If you've got a spreadsheet of donors, you are not typing them in one by one.

26
00:01:17,709 --> 00:01:24,020
GrantPipe imports four kinds of records: contacts, donations, grants, and grant opportunities.

27
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:25,362
Start with contacts.

28
00:01:25,362 --> 00:01:30,599
That's the donor list almost everyone already has sitting in a file somewhere.

29
00:01:30,599 --> 00:01:35,231
Came out of Bloomerang, DonorPerfect, or Salesforce's nonprofit pack?

30
00:01:35,231 --> 00:01:38,587
Pick that preset, and the columns line up for you.

31
00:01:38,587 --> 00:01:43,018
Anything else, like a plain Excel sheet or an old Blackbaud export

32
00:01:43,018 --> 00:01:46,375
choose Generic CSV and match the columns yourself.

33
00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:47,180
It's simple.

34
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:50,000
And you can download a template to follow.

35
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,217
Upload your file.

36
00:01:51,217 --> 00:01:53,221
Any CSV up to ten megabytes.

37
00:01:53,221 --> 00:01:55,727
Then GrantPipe shows you a preview.

38
00:01:55,727 --> 00:01:58,733
Here's the part I want you to relax about.

39
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:00,022
This is a preview.

40
00:02:00,022 --> 00:02:01,024
Nothing saves.

41
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:05,534
GrantPipe reads your file and tells you how many rows it found.

42
00:02:05,534 --> 00:02:11,547
It shows you the first five, so you can check the columns landed where you expected.

43
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:15,770
If something looks wrong, back out and fix the spreadsheet.

44
00:02:15,770 --> 00:02:18,132
Nothing has touched your account.

45
00:02:18,132 --> 00:02:19,278
That's the idea.

46
00:02:19,278 --> 00:02:21,640
You look before anything happens.

47
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:24,368
When the preview looks right, you commit.

48
00:02:24,368 --> 00:02:29,758
Now GrantPipe writes the records, and it tells you the truth about what happened.

49
00:02:29,758 --> 00:02:30,689
Three numbers.

50
00:02:30,689 --> 00:02:31,355
Two added.

51
00:02:31,355 --> 00:02:33,218
Three skipped as duplicates.

52
00:02:33,218 --> 00:02:39,406
When GrantPipe spots a contact you already have, it skips it instead of making a second copy.

53
00:02:39,406 --> 00:02:43,997
And one failed, with the line and the field, so you know what to fix.

54
00:02:43,997 --> 00:02:45,927
A few rows failing is normal.

55
00:02:45,927 --> 00:02:52,314
Here, line seven used a contact type GrantPipe doesn't know, so it tells you the two it accepts.

56
00:02:52,314 --> 00:02:57,038
A blank required field or a date it can't read fail the same clear way.

57
00:02:57,038 --> 00:02:59,700
Fix those lines and re-import just them.

58
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,892
GrantPipe logs every import you run right here, with its counts

59
00:03:03,892 --> 00:03:07,086
so you've got a record of what came in and when.

60
00:03:07,086 --> 00:03:08,350
And there they are.

61
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:10,080
Your donors, in GrantPipe.

62
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:12,986
That's your org set up and your data in.

63
00:03:12,986 --> 00:03:17,635
No consultant, no migration project, and nobody had to dread it.

64
00:03:17,635 --> 00:03:19,233
One tip before you go.

65
00:03:19,233 --> 00:03:24,972
If your spreadsheet is messy, and most are, map your columns before you import.

66
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:29,403
We made a free template for that: the CRM Migration Data Map.

67
00:03:29,403 --> 00:03:34,053
It tells you which column goes where and what to clean up first.

68
00:03:34,053 --> 00:03:34,634
Want it?

69
00:03:34,634 --> 00:03:36,668
We'll send it to your inbox.

70
00:03:36,668 --> 00:03:41,317
Next, we'll add your first grant and split it across your funds.

71
00:03:41,317 --> 00:03:45,240
That's where GrantPipe really starts earning its keep.
